From de44773737ad7a6865bd820f042d38785d0171eb Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kara Zajac <kara@soulstone.org>
Date: Thu, 1 Oct 2026 08:46:57 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] maki: under --realtime, an idle core waits for the host's
 clock rather than leaping to its next timer

Asleep with nothing pending, the core skipped straight to its next timer's deadline, however far,
and --realtime then held the whole emulator until this computer's clock caught up, the host's
bytes waiting meanwhile: a minute or more per round trip. On a badge, USB's interrupt would wake
it at once. Now the Machine has an idle limit (emu_set_idle_limit) an asleep core skips no
further than, and emu_run returns there; shot holds it a few milliseconds ahead of the host's
clock, sleeps that long, and wakes maki for the host's bytes. Paced, a slice counts the time it
took (10 ns each), so presses, answers and profiles keep their lengths in time.

A store install of an 87 KB app: about 25 s, from 12 minutes for one of 248 KB.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5.5 <noreply@anthropic.com>
Claude-Session: https://claude.ai/code/session_01KrrQLcb6MfwT6Kc7SresSY
